# 7. ATTENDANCE CALCULATION RULES

Use centralized business logic.

Define:

- Scheduled start time
- Scheduled end time
- Check-in time
- Check-out time
- Working duration
- Late threshold
- Half-day threshold
- Approved leave
- Holiday
- Weekend

Calculate attendance status consistently.

Handle:

- Missing check-in
- Missing check-out
- Late check-in
- Early check-out
- Overnight shifts if supported
- Approved leave
- Holidays
- Weekends
- Timezone changes

Use local date keys for date-only attendance records.

Avoid accidental calendar-day shifts caused by UTC conversion.

Do not assume every employee works the same schedule.

---

# 8. LEAVE BALANCE CALCULATION

Implement:

Opening Balance
+ Accrued Leave
+ Approved Adjustments
− Approved Leave Usage
= Available Leave Balance

Clearly define how pending requests affect the displayed balance.

Use working-day calculations.

Exclude configured weekends and holidays where appropriate.

Handle partial-day leave only if explicitly supported by the data model.

Prevent duplicate deductions.

If an approved leave request is cancelled, restore the balance exactly once.

Do not claim the demo policies comply with employment law.

---

# 9. PAYROLL CALCULATION ENGINE

Use decimal.js or integer minor units.

Avoid floating-point precision errors.

Example:

Gross Pay = Base Pay + Allowances + Bonuses

Total Deductions = Configured Deductions + Applicable Unpaid Leave Adjustments

Net Pay = Gross Pay − Total Deductions

Define rounding rules.

Support:

- Monthly payroll
- Base salary
- Allowances
- Bonuses
- Deductions
- Unpaid leave adjustments
- Payroll preview
- Finalized snapshot
- Payslip export

This is a simplified simulation.

Do not implement assumed tax withholding, statutory contributions, overtime law, or legal compliance unless explicitly configurable and verified.

Do not process real payments.

